    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research a generic node-ID index to replace repeated linear `find_by_id` scans. Support arbitrary IDs and preserve first-match behavior if duplicates are admitted; ignore missing neighbor IDs as the source does. Preserve neighbor order (it affects ties) and the original query-token spelling and order in output. Measure whole-program runtime, not just lookup time.\",\"B\":\"Research computing the source’s recursive path sums only once per node, using memoization or an iterative DAG evaluation to avoid repeated path work and deep recursion. Preserve the exact rule: choose the first neighbor with the largest strictly positive node value—not the neighbor with the largest path sum—and add its recursively computed result. Unknown query IDs return zero; keep float accumulation behavior. Validate correctness and measure end-to-end.\",\"C\":\"Explore whether the four CPU cores help: after determining each node’s exact selected successor, evaluate independent ready nodes in parallel and store results for ordered output. Avoid concurrent tokenization or printing, and compare with a serial version; thread and scheduling overhead may outweigh gains on this workload. Stay within the existing toolchain and do not specialize to test inputs.\",\"G\":\"Treat A, B, and C as competing research results, not additive improvements. For each candidate and any proposed combination, use the fixed harness for correctness and comparable timing; retain only an actually measured improvement. Preserve the source’s selection, tie, missing-ID, formatting, and query-order semantics for every valid input. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions; if no valid faster candidate is demonstrated, retain the parent baseline.\",\"analysis\":\"The official problem is graph search on a DAG; the supplied source performs linear ID lookup and recursively recomputes selected paths. Its selection semantics are important: it follows the first neighbor with the greatest strictly positive node value, irrespective of that neighbor’s eventual path sum. The baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.0118766101 s across three timings (0.0119770989, 0.0116870888, 0.0119656427 s), score 0.5. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPUs. These measurements motivate experiments but do not establish that any individual optimization or combination will improve end-to-end time.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"c3afb0d74ef4e2c5bf7cff4f5c732b33c44ebcb34fe3d03f7c2ad5dfc17807ac\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research a generic node-ID index to replace repeated linear `find_by_id` scans. Support arbitrary IDs and preserve first-match behavior if duplicates are admitted; ignore missing neighbor IDs as the source does. Preserve neighbor order (it affects ties) and the original query-token spelling and order in output. Measure whole-program runtime, not just lookup time.\",\"B\":\"Research computing the source’s recursive path sums only once per node, using memoization or an iterative DAG evaluation to avoid repeated path work and deep recursion. Preserve the exact rule: choose the first neighbor with the largest strictly positive node value—not the neighbor with the largest path sum—and add its recursively computed result. Unknown query IDs return zero; keep float accumulation behavior. Validate correctness and measure end-to-end.\",\"C\":\"Explore whether the four CPU cores help: after determining each node’s exact selected successor, evaluate independent ready nodes in parallel and store results for ordered output. Avoid concurrent tokenization or printing, and compare with a serial version; thread and scheduling overhead may outweigh gains on this workload. Stay within the existing toolchain and do not specialize to test inputs.\",\"G\":\"Treat A, B, and C as competing research results, not additive improvements. For each candidate and any proposed combination, use the fixed harness for correctness and comparable timing; retain only an actually measured improvement. Preserve the source’s selection, tie, missing-ID, formatting, and query-order semantics for every valid input. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions; if no valid faster candidate is demonstrated, retain the parent baseline.\",\"analysis\":\"The official problem is graph search on a DAG; the supplied source performs linear ID lookup and recursively recomputes selected paths. Its selection semantics are important: it follows the first neighbor with the greatest strictly positive node value, irrespective of that neighbor’s eventual path sum. The baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.0118766101 s across three timings (0.0119770989, 0.0116870888, 0.0119656427 s), score 0.5. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPUs. These measurements motivate experiments but do not establish that any individual optimization or combination will improve end-to-end time.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"32411b2b3aa74d879222222a71646882\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"c3afb0d74ef4e2c5bf7cff4f5c732b33c44ebcb34fe3d03f7c2ad5dfc17807ac\",\"session_id\":\"bb7a58941e74486ba24267f2f526de8f\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"b2d0c755f6afd7bd5086d1adf646e891497efa6827d6f697c0b8e869db24b3b6\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":11983,\"output_tokens\":4854,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":4380}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":16837,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
